Return and Backspace in code keep the indentation (round 19).

=== Return keeps the line's indentation
--- before
#% cell
f(x) := {
    a = 1▮
--- keys
Enter "b"
--- after
#% cell
f(x) := {
    a = 1
    b▮

=== Return after an opening bracket indents one level more
--- before
#% cell
g = table(▮
--- keys
Enter "x"
--- after
#% cell
g = table(
    x▮

=== Return between a bracket and its closer puts the closer on its own line
--- before
#% cell
f(x) := {▮}
--- keys
Enter "y"
--- after
#% cell
f(x) := {
    y▮
}

=== the closer line keeps the outer indentation
--- before
#% cell
    h = [▮]
--- keys
Enter "1"
--- after
#% cell
    h = [
        1▮
    ]

=== with automatic closing, typing an opener then Return splits the pair
--- before
#% cell
k = ▮
--- keys
AutoClose "{" Enter "a"
--- after
#% cell
k = {
    a▮
}

=== Return inside a string is a plain line break
--- before
#% cell
    s = "a(▮"
--- keys
Enter
--- after
#% cell
    s = "a(
▮"

=== Return in a Text cell keeps no indentation
--- before
#% text
    indented prose▮
--- keys
Enter "b"
--- after
#% text
    indented prose
b▮

=== Backspace in leading spaces goes back to the previous indentation stop
--- before
#% cell
a
        ▮b
--- keys
Backspace
--- after
#% cell
a
    ▮b

=== Backspace in an odd indentation goes to the stop below
--- before
#% cell
a
      ▮b
--- keys
Backspace
--- after
#% cell
a
    ▮b

=== Backspace after a single space deletes one space
--- before
#% cell
a
 ▮b
--- keys
Backspace
--- after
#% cell
a
▮b

=== Backspace after text deletes one character
--- before
#% cell
    x  ▮
--- keys
Backspace
--- after
#% cell
    x ▮

=== undo of a Return with indentation is one step
--- before
#% cell
f(▮)
--- keys
Enter Ctrl+Z
--- after
#% cell
f(▮)
